window.DDIA = window.DDIA || {chapters:[], viz:[], system:null};
window.DDIA.chapters.push({
  n: 4, title: "Storage and Retrieval",
  worksheet: "https://docs.google.com/document/d/1-IMPKDsHbg3JiN29WQjoR4SfCFL9n0wriXcz69UllqA/edit",
  why: "Chapter 3 was <i>what shape</i> to store. This one is <i>what the disk does</i> when you store it, and it's where \"Cassandra for the messages table, Postgres for the rest\" stops being a preference and becomes a sentence about write amplification. The follow-up \"why is that write fast\" or \"why is that query slow\" lands here every time.",
  jargon: [
    ["Storage engine", "The part of the database that decides how bytes land on disk and how they're found again. Two families: log-structured (append, compact later) and page-oriented (fixed-size pages updated in place)."],
    ["Index", "An extra structure derived from the data that makes some lookups fast and every write slower. The whole chapter is the tradeoff in that sentence."],
    ["Hash index", "An in-memory map from key to byte offset in an append-only log (Bitcask). One seek per lookup; every key must fit in RAM; no range queries."],
    ["Byte offset", "How many bytes from the start of the log file a record begins. The hash map holds the offset, not the value, so values can be large."],
    ["SSTable", "<b>Sorted String Table</b>: a file of key-value pairs sorted by key, each key once. Merging two is a linear streaming pass; a sparse index finds any key with one seek."],
    ["Memtable", "The in-memory sorted structure new writes go into before being flushed as an SSTable."],
    ["LSM tree", "<b>Log-Structured Merge tree</b>: write-ahead log → memtable → immutable SSTables → compaction. Turns random writes into sequential appends and pays later with compaction."],
    ["Compaction", "Merging SSTables so there are fewer to check and dead versions are dropped. Size-tiered (merge same-size files, cheap writes), leveled (non-overlapping ranges per level, cheap reads), time-window (only within a time bucket; old files never rewritten)."],
    ["Tombstone", "A marker appended to say a key is deleted. In an append-only store, forgetting is a write. Dropped at compaction."],
    ["Bloom filter", "A bit array with k hashes per key. \"Definitely not here\" or \"maybe\". Never a false negative; false positives at a tunable rate (~1% at 10 bits/key). Can't delete, so it belongs to something immutable."],
    ["B-tree", "Pages arranged as a shallow tree, each holding sorted keys and child pointers; a lookup is 3–4 page reads. Updates in place. Postgres, MySQL, every relational default."],
    ["Write-ahead log (WAL)", "An append-only log a B-tree writes to <i>before</i> touching a page, so a crash mid-write (a torn page) can be repaired. Every write happens twice."],
    ["Write / read / space amplification", "Bytes hitting disk per byte the app wrote · files or pages touched per lookup · extra disk held by old versions and in-progress compaction."],
    ["Clustered index", "The rows live inside the index, sorted by its key (InnoDB, Cassandra's partition + clustering key). Postgres does <i>not</i>: rows live in a heap and indexes point into it."],
    ["Heap file", "Rows stored in no particular order, appended where there's room; indexes point at them by location. (Not the memory heap, not the priority-queue heap.)"],
    ["Secondary index", "An index on a non-key column, of the same family as the engine, mapping value → primary key. On an LSM engine a mutable indexed column forces a read before every write."],
    ["Covering index", "A secondary index carrying the extra columns a query needs, so the query never touches the row. Postgres: <code>CREATE INDEX … INCLUDE (cols)</code>; the plan reads Index Only Scan."],
    ["Composite key", "A primary key of several columns, compared column by column (or encoded into one order-preserving byte string on LSM engines). Usable only from the left: a prefix works, a suffix alone doesn't."],
    ["Partition key / clustering column", "Cassandra's names for the two halves of <code>(conv_id, seq)</code>: which node and region holds the rows; the order within it."],
    ["OLTP / OLAP", "Many small reads and writes by key · few queries scanning millions of rows. Different storage: row-oriented with indexes vs column-oriented."],
    ["Column-oriented storage", "Each column's values stored together. Scans touch only the columns they need; columns compress 10–100× (bitmap, run-length); one sort order per copy; writes are batched in, never updated in place."],
    ["Star schema / fact table", "A wide fact table (one row per event) surrounded by dimension tables. The fact table is what the cube is rebuilt from."],
    ["Materialized view / data cube", "A query result stored as a real table and refreshed · a materialized view of aggregates over a few dimensions. Drills down only along the dimensions it was built with."],
    ["Vector index", "An approximate nearest-neighbour structure over embeddings (HNSW, IVF). Trades recall for speed; fights with filters and updates; inherits every flaw of the model that made the vectors."]
  ],
  sections: [
    { id: "recap", title: "Recap questions", intro: "Answered from memory of the chapter, then corrected.", items: [
      { q: "What does a B-tree do differently on a write, and why does that make it slower to write but faster to read?",
        robby: "\"Self-rebalancing, so one write can kick off writes down the depth of the tree.\" Splits are rare; a page holds hundreds of keys.",
        a: "<p>Update in place versus append. The write cost is: <b>whole pages, not values</b> (a 100-byte change rewrites a 4–8 KB page, 40–80× amplification on every write); <b>the write-ahead log</b> (every write hits disk twice); <b>random writes</b> (pages are wherever they are; the LSM turns the same workload into sequential appends). Splits are the rare fourth.</p><p>Reads are faster not because the tree is shallow (SSTables have sparse indexes too) but because <b>every key lives in exactly one place</b>: 3–4 page reads to one leaf, done. An LSM lookup checks the memtable then each SSTable newest-first, a miss checks all of them, and compaction steals disk bandwidth at unpredictable moments.</p>",
        line: "a B-tree pays on writes by rewriting whole pages twice, in random places; an LSM tree pays on reads by having to look in several places." },
      { q: "A key that doesn't exist: how expensive in an LSM tree, and what fixes it?",
        a: "<p>Every SSTable that could hold it, newest to oldest. Size-tiered: every file. Leveled: one file per level, since a level's ranges don't overlap and a binary search picks the single candidate. \"Keep a list of all keys\" is the hash-index design and dies when keys exceed RAM. The fix is a <b>Bloom filter per SSTable</b>: \"definitely not here\" skips the file, \"maybe\" opens it and is wrong ~1% of the time. It helps existing keys too (skip the files a present key isn't in) and does nothing for ranges.</p>" },
      { q: "Why not one Bloom filter for the whole database?",
        a: "<p><b>Deletes.</b> A filter can't remove a key (clearing a bit may clear it for another key). Per-file filters dodge this because the file is immutable: built once, discarded with the file at compaction. A global filter accumulates every key compaction ever removed until it says \"maybe\" to everything, and the only fix is a full rebuild. Then <b>size</b> (10 bits/key at 3.65T keys/year ≈ 4.5 TB, which must be in RAM) and <b>payoff</b> (only true misses benefit; in chat almost every lookup exists). The hierarchy goes <i>down</i>: per-block filters inside a file, and prefix filters on <code>conv_id</code> for range scans.</p>",
        line: "a Bloom filter belongs to something immutable and bounded." },
      { q: "The messages table is keyed <code>(conv_id, seq)</code>. Which family wants it, and why does the column order matter?",
        robby: "\"Read-heavy, because the client looks messages up to display them — B-tree.\" The ratio and the shape are the test, not who reads.",
        a: "<p><b>LSM.</b> 600k inbox writes/s average, 3M peak, every one an append never updated; reads are of the newest data, served from the memtable or the newest file. \"Read-heavy\" (5:1) with reads concentrated on what just arrived is a write-heavy table whose reads are nearly free. B-trees earn their place on tables that get <i>updated</i>: participants, conversations.</p><p>Column order: both families sort by key, so order decides what sits next to what. <code>(conv_id, seq)</code> makes a conversation one contiguous run in order — \"last 50 in X\" is one range read. <code>(seq, conv_id)</code> would put X's 1041 next to Y's 1041, fifty scattered lookups. In Cassandra, <code>conv_id</code> is the partition key, <code>seq</code> the clustering column.</p>" },
      { q: "Why would \"average messages per user per day for 2025\" be slow on the messages table, and what makes it fast?",
        robby: "\"Build a secondary index on date.\" An index helps find needles; \"all of 2025\" is the haystack.",
        a: "<p>3.65 trillion rows, and a row-oriented store reads the whole row (body, attachments) to look at a timestamp — petabytes read to use terabytes — on the table serving 3M writes/s. A date index would walk you to every row in scan order plus cost a write per send. <b>Column-oriented storage in a separate warehouse</b>: a copy fed from the message events, minutes stale; the scan reads only the sender and date columns, each compressed enormously (bitmap, run-length); a star schema with one fact row per send. Then the <b>cube</b>: <code>(user, day) → count</code>, incremented as events arrive, so the query is 365B small rows instead of 3.65T wide ones — and it answers only the questions you built it for.</p>" },
      { q: "Both a column store and a cube, or just the cube if the question is stable?",
        a: "<p>Both. The cube is a projection and needs something to project <i>from</i>: a new question has no source, a correction (late event, counter bug) has no source, and \"the consistent query\" never stays consistent. The fact table is cheap: two or three narrow columns per message, compressed a hundredfold, parked as columnar files in object storage (the data lake section). Cube-only is right only when the source is genuinely too big to keep, said out loud so product knows they're buying \"this number can never be recomputed.\"</p>" },
      { q: "Isn't the columnar copy 2× the storage?",
        a: "<p>No — you copy the columns analytics can use, and the expensive column is the one it can't. A ~1 KB message row is ~300 B body, ~100 B attachment metadata, and ~30 B of sender, conversation, timestamp, kind. The analytics copy is 3% before compression, then 10–100× smaller, then in object storage at cloud prices: tens of terabytes for a 4 PB year. Rule: keep every cheap narrow column, drop every wide one. And never the body, for a stronger reason than cost: a warehouse with bodies is a privacy incident waiting for a query.</p>" },
      { q: "What happens to a secondary index on <code>sender_id</code> in an LSM tree, and why is that worse than in a B-tree?",
        robby: "\"Point at levels, not memory chunks, and search more data.\" Made up on the spot — and the pointer-to-file instinct was half right for the wrong reason.",
        a: "<p>A secondary index is a second copy of the <b>same family</b>, mapping index key → <b>primary key</b> (not a location: in an LSM the row moves at every compaction). Two lookups either way. <b>B-tree</b>: the value changes → delete the old entry, insert the new, in place; splits don't matter because entries point by primary key. <b>LSM</b>: can't delete in place, so the old index entry sits in an older file pointing at a row that no longer matches. Two ways out, both bad: <b>read before write</b> (fetch the old value, tombstone its entry — the blind append the LSM exists for is gone) or <b>verify on read</b> (every index hit fetches the row to check). The twist: a message's sender never changes, so on the messages table it's just a second append per send, the cheap version — but in Cassandra indexes are per node (scatter-gather), so you'd build a <code>(sender_id, timestamp)</code> table yourself instead.</p>",
        line: "a secondary index is a second tree of the same family pointing at primary keys; on an LSM a mutable indexed column forces a read before every write." },
      { q: "Would an update-heavy whiteboard justify a B-tree over an LSM?",
        a: "<p>Right conclusion, wrong reason. \"Mostly updates\" alone doesn't push you to a B-tree: an LSM treats an update as an append. What tips it is <b>a small hot set of keys rewritten constantly</b>: the LSM fills with stale versions of the same few keys and compaction spends its life rewriting live data to clear them; the B-tree rewrites one page and the old version is gone. Two follow-ups make it a design answer: a whiteboard fits in memory, so the engine is nearly moot (disk is for durability); and real collaborative whiteboards are event logs of strokes anyway, for ordering, conflict resolution, and undo — the durable store is append-only after all, and the board is a projection.</p>",
        line: "update-in-place wins when a small set of keys churns and readers need the latest value now." },
      { q: "Does a secondary index always mirror the primary's family? B-tree index over an LSM table?",
        a: "<p>Inside one database, always the same family, and the reason is <b>atomicity</b>: the index must change in the same write as the row, and only the same engine, same log, same transaction gives that. Mixing happens <i>across</i> systems, and then it isn't an index, it's a copy: Cassandra messages + Elasticsearch search index (LSM-shaped too, as it happens); Postgres participants + a columnar fact table; a per-sender table written by the app. An index is updated in the write; a copy is updated after it.</p>",
        line: "an index shares its engine's family because it shares its transaction; a copy in another system can be any family, and you pay for the freedom with lag." },
      { q: "A widely used system that wants a B-tree more than an LSM?",
        a: "<p>Anything where the unit of data is a <b>balance</b> or a <b>state</b>, not an event: bank ledger balances (Stripe, Venmo), inventory (Amazon, Shopify), reservations and seats (Ticketmaster, Resy), every app's <code>users</code> table, session and cart state. Few rows, rewritten constantly, every read must see the latest write, transactions across rows. A message is an event: it happened once and is never unhappened. A balance is a fact about now; its history is a separate ledger, append-only, happy as an LSM. Most real systems have both tables. Most applications are mostly state, which is why Postgres and MySQL are \"the most popular database.\"</p>",
        line: "events go to the LSM, current state goes to the B-tree; your application wants the state and writes the events; your analysts want a copy of the events." }
    ]},
    { id: "tradeoffs", title: "Mechanisms as tradeoffs", items: [
      { q: "Append-only log with an in-memory hash index",
        robby: "\"That's LSM.\" It's the design <i>before</i> the LSM tree: no sorting. And \"breaks on hot rows\" is backwards — hot rows are its best case.",
        a: "<p>Bitcask. Every write appends; the hash map moves the offset; old segments are compacted to drop dead versions. Update and delete are appends too: a delete is a tombstone record (so a restart's log scan doesn't resurrect the key) plus dropping the key from the map.</p><pre>log:  byte 0  key=42 val=\"ada\"     byte 58  key=42 val=\"adb\"     byte 89  key=42 TOMBSTONE\nmap:  17 → 31   (42 removed)</pre>",
        when: "Point lookups only; the key set is bounded and fits in RAM (40 bytes × 1B keys = 40 GB fits one machine, so not \"a few million\"); values as big as you like; keys updated often. Caches, session stores, a URL shortener's slug map.",
        breaks: ["<b>More distinct keys than RAM.</b>", "<b>Range queries.</b> A hash map has no order. This is the gap sorting the log (the SSTable) exists to close.", "<b>Crash recovery.</b> The map is in RAM; a restart re-reads the whole log unless the map is snapshotted."],
        line: "the fastest possible point store for a key set that fits in memory; it breaks on range queries and on more keys than RAM, which is exactly what sorting the log fixes." },
      { q: "LSM tree",
        robby: "\"Breaks when appending and deleting a lot.\" Appending is what it's best at; a delete is one more append.",
        when: "High write volume, mostly new keys, reads that favour recent data or scan ranges. Logs, events, messages, metrics, time series. Sorting the log buys a sparse index (keys needn't fit RAM), range queries, and cheap merging at once.",
        breaks: ["<b>Reads that miss or go deep.</b> Bloom filters cover the miss; only leveled compaction keeps the file count per level low for old data.", "<b>Compaction competing with the foreground.</b> It shares the disk with your queries; p99 rises when it runs.", "<b>Writes outrunning compaction.</b> Files grow without bound, every read slows, the disk fills with stale versions; the engine doesn't throttle you, it just gets worse until it falls over.", "<b>A small hot set churning</b> (the whiteboard): compaction spends its life rewriting the same live keys.", "<b>Transactions.</b> A key exists in several places at once; a B-tree has each key in one place, which locking leans on."],
        line: "choose it for append-heavy workloads with reads of recent data; it breaks when reads go deep or miss, when compaction can't keep up or steals the disk, and when you need one-place-per-key." },
      { q: "B-tree",
        robby: "\"Bad for history — you don't have the original after an edit — and you can't replay the log to rebuild views.\" Neither engine keeps history; that's the data model's job. And range queries are a strength, not a worst case.",
        when: "State that changes in place and must read back exactly, at moderate write rates. Balances, inventory, users, sessions. Leaves in key order make range scans a walk of neighbouring pages with nothing to merge.",
        breaks: ["<b>Write cost.</b> Whole page, random place, twice (log + page). Millions of appends a second means rewriting 8 KB to change 100 bytes.", "<b>Slack.</b> Splits leave half-empty pages (sequential inserts especially); deletes leave holes; never reclaimed without a rebuild.", "<b>Concurrency.</b> In-place updates need page latches; readers-don't-block-writers needs multi-version rows and a vacuum — the B-tree's own garbage tax.", "<b>Crash mid-page.</b> A torn page; the write-ahead log exists to repair it."],
        line: "B-tree for state that changes in place and must read back exactly; it breaks on append-heavy volume, dead space, and the locking in-place updates force. History and replay are the data model's job." },
      { q: "Size-tiered compaction (the toy-box rule)",
        a: "<p>Four small files → one medium; four mediums → one large. Files grouped by size; nothing else controlled, so same-size files can overlap.</p>",
        when: "Write-heavy, append-mostly: each entry is rewritten a handful of times in its life, once per tier. Reads of recent data (the small, newest files). You can spare the disk. Cassandra's historical default.",
        breaks: ["<b>Read amplification.</b> A key can be in any file; a miss or a scan opens everything overlapping.", "<b>Space amplification.</b> Merging four large files needs room for inputs and output: briefly 2×; near a full disk the compaction that would free space can't run.", "<b>Stale versions live long.</b> A large-tier file may not be merged for days.", "<b>Big, rare compactions.</b> The disk hit lands as a spike."],
        line: "the cheapest write path, paid for in read fan-out, temporary double disk, and long-lived stale versions." },
      { q: "Leveled compaction (the shelf rule)",
        robby: "\"Cheaper reads, cost more to write\" — also how someone describes a B-tree. The mechanism is the row.",
        a: "<p>Levels each ~10× the one above, <i>same file size, more files</i>. Within a level below L0, files have non-overlapping key ranges. When a level is over budget, move one file down by merging it into only the files it overlaps below (found by binary search over the sorted ranges). Nothing else is re-sorted — but one file overlaps ~10 below, so moving S bytes rewrites ~10 S; across 5–6 levels each entry is rewritten ~50 times.</p>",
        when: "Point reads and range scans on data of any age (at most one file per level, 5–6 files worst case, regardless of size). Tight disk (one file moves at a time, a few percent overhead). Stale versions and tombstones must clear quickly. RocksDB and LevelDB default.",
        breaks: ["<b>Write amplification</b>, ten-to-one per level.", "<b>Compaction never stops</b>: a steady hum that permanently takes a slice of disk bandwidth.", "<b>Falling behind cascades.</b> If L1 can't drain, L0 (the one overlapping level) fills and reads degrade fast; RocksDB stalls writes outright."],
        line: "bounded reads and tight disk, paid with ten-to-one rewrites at every level; right for tables read as much as written, wrong for a pure append firehose." },
      { q: "Bloom filter in front of the SSTables",
        when: "Point lookups where misses are common or files are many: unique-insert checks, \"does this user exist,\" cache-miss paths, any size-tiered store. ~10 bits/key in RAM for 1%. It helps hits too: a key in the third-newest file skips the first two.",
        breaks: ["<b>Range queries.</b> \"Between A and C\" can't be hashed; a scan opens every overlapping file.", "<b>Undersized.</b> Too few bits and the false-positive rate climbs while the memory cost stays.", "<b>Memory at scale.</b> Per-block filters loaded on demand, and prefix filters for the range case."],
        line: "a per-file \"definitely not here\" check; helps hits and misses alike, does nothing for ranges, and is only as good as the bits you gave it." },
      { q: "Clustered index (rows live inside the index)",
        when: "The dominant query is a lookup or range on the primary key and wants the whole row. Find the key, the data is in the leaf. <code>(conv_id, seq)</code> is the textbook case. InnoDB always; Cassandra's partition + clustering; Postgres not by default (heap + pointing indexes).",
        breaks: ["<b>Secondary indexes get a second hop.</b> Rows move on splits, so secondaries store the primary key; a wide primary key makes every secondary wide.", "<b>Only one physical order.</b> Pick the hot range query; everything else pays the double hop.", "<b>Random-order inserts</b> (a UUID primary key) split pages and shuffle rows; sequential keys append.", "<b>Wide rows</b> mean few per page, taller trees, more pages per scan — the covering-index row's problem."],
        line: "cluster on the key your hot range query uses; every other lookup pays a second hop and you only get one physical order." },
      { q: "Secondary index on a write-heavy table",
        robby: "\"Breaks when writes arrive in order, because the index equals the primary key order.\" True only when the indexed column is monotonic with the primary key (timestamp when you already have seq): don't index a column the primary key already orders.",
        when: "The query on the second column is frequent, selective, and the column doesn't change. On the messages table the column is immutable, so it's a second append per send.",
        breaks: ["<b>It multiplies the write.</b> Three indexes on 3M writes/s is 12M writes/s; the disk doesn't care that nine are \"just indexes.\"", "<b>Mutable column on an LSM engine</b>: read before write, stale entry lingers. The stepper preset.", "<b>Low selectivity.</b> \"All of 2025\" touches the whole table plus the index.", "<b>Hot index keys.</b> A broadcast sender's million messages under one key is one hot partition.", "<b>Scatter-gather.</b> Cassandra's secondaries are per node; build the second table yourself."],
        line: "a secondary index is a second table you don't get to design, written on every insert; pay for it only when the column is immutable and the query is selective and hot." },
      { q: "Covering index",
        a: "<p>The same idea as \"write the second table yourself,\" one rung down: the engine does the copy for you, atomically, restricted to the base table's partitioning and to columns rather than a new shape. Postgres: <code>CREATE INDEX participants_inbox ON participants (user_id, last_activity DESC) INCLUDE (conv_id, preview);</code> — key columns sorted and filtered on, <code>INCLUDE</code> columns stored as payload; <code>EXPLAIN</code> shows Index Only Scan. SQL Server coined the term; MySQL puts the columns in the key (every InnoDB secondary carries the primary key anyway); Cassandra has none. Postgres wrinkle: index-only scans still check row visibility via the visibility map, which is stale on heavily updated tables — so they pay off on read-mostly tables.</p>",
        when: "A hot query filters on a secondary key and reads a few columns: the inbox reading <code>(user_id, last_activity) → conv_id, preview</code> never touches a participant row.",
        breaks: ["<b>Every write now writes those columns twice.</b>", "<b>A covered column that changes</b> rewrites the index entry — read-before-write on an LSM.", "<b>One query shape.</b> The next screen wants one more column.", "<b>Bloat.</b> Cover three columns, not twelve."],
        line: "include the columns the hot query reads so it never touches the row; pay for it on every write, and cover only what one query needs." },
      { q: "Keeping the whole dataset in memory",
        robby: "\"Snapshot to cloud storage in case it crashes\" — cloud object storage is a backup, minutes to restore, not a recovery path for a serving node.",
        a: "<p>Why it's faster isn't \"no disk seeks\" — the OS page cache already serves hot pages from RAM. It's <b>not having to encode data in a disk-friendly layout</b>: sorted sets, priority queues, graphs, pointers, no serialization. Redis's data types are that argument made into a product. Durability is a choice: append-only log on local disk (Redis AOF), periodic snapshots to shorten replay (RDB), replication to another machine's memory so a crash fails over instead of restoring.</p>",
        when: "The working set is small next to RAM and reads are latency-critical or need rich structures: caches, session state, rate limiters, leaderboards, presence, the whiteboard, the people-you-may-know list.",
        breaks: ["<b>Bigger than RAM</b> (anti-caching evicts cold data to disk, and now you've rebuilt a disk database badly).", "<b>Restart time</b>: rebuilding 200 GB from log + snapshot is downtime; hence replicated pairs.", "<b>Cost</b>: RAM is 10–20× SSD per GB.", "<b>Pauses</b>: a big heap in a garbage-collected runtime stops the world."],
        line: "in memory when the working set fits and the structures matter more than the bytes; durable through a log and a replica, not through hope." },
      { q: "Column-oriented storage",
        a: "<p>Why it compresses: a column is one type with few distinct values relative to rows, long runs if sorted. <b>Bitmap encoding</b> (one bit-vector per distinct value) plus <b>run-length encoding</b>; the CPU ANDs compressed bitmaps for a <code>WHERE</code> without decompressing, in tight loops over dense arrays. <b>Sort order</b>: one column to sort all rows by, every column file written in that order — a range on it is one contiguous stretch, and it compresses to near nothing; one sort order per copy, so Vertica keeps replicas sorted differently.</p>",
        when: "Scans reading a few columns across most rows: aggregates, group-bys, dashboards. Data that arrives in bulk and is never updated in place.",
        breaks: ["<b>Writes.</b> You can't insert one row into a compressed sorted column file; writes go to an in-memory row buffer and merge in bulk, minutes later (the LSM approach again).", "<b>Whole-row reads.</b> One seek per column file and reassemble; point lookups are what it's worst at.", "<b>Columns that don't compress</b>: high-cardinality, unsorted, free text. The message body would be its worst tenant.", "<b>The column you didn't keep.</b> Cheap narrow columns are free to keep, so keep them."],
        line: "store each column together so a scan reads only what it needs and compresses what it reads; breaks on point reads and anything written one row at a time." },
      { q: "Materialized view or data cube",
        a: "<p>A materialized view is any query result stored as a real table and <i>refreshed</i> — on every write, on a schedule, or on demand; \"how stale is it\" is the whole tradeoff. A cube is a materialized view of aggregates over dimensions (date across the top, vendor down the side, totals in the corner), drillable only along the dimensions it was built with.</p>",
        when: "You know the questions ahead of time and ask them daily: money per customer, per day, top customers. Incremented as events land, or recomputed nightly.",
        breaks: ["<b>The question you didn't precompute.</b>", "<b>Dimensions multiply</b>: five dimensions at a thousand values each is 10<sup>15</sup> cells, and the cube can never answer \"<i>which</i> invoice.\"", "<b>Refresh cost or staleness</b>, and late events or corrections.", "<b>It's a cache</b>: only as right as the source, and unrebuildable without the fact table."],
        line: "precompute the aggregates you'll ask for daily along the few dimensions you'll slice by; keep the fact table, because the cube can't answer the question you didn't build." },
      { q: "Approximate vector index",
        robby: "Described the embedding (\"how LLMs understand what people are saying\"). The row is about the index that makes searching a billion embeddings possible.",
        a: "<p>An embedding model turns text or an image into a vector of ~1000 numbers where similar meaning lands nearby. The query is \"the k nearest vectors to this one.\" Exact is a linear scan; at a billion vectors, no sorted order helps because \"near\" in a thousand dimensions isn't a range on any axis — which is why the chapter files it with the multidimensional (geospatial) indexes. <b>HNSW</b>: a graph where each vector links to neighbours with express links on top; <b>IVF</b>: cluster, then search only the nearest clusters. Both return an approximation.</p>",
        when: "Semantic search, recommendations, \"images like this,\" near-duplicate detection, retrieval for a language model. Anywhere the question is \"what's <i>like</i> this\" rather than \"what <i>is</i> this.\"",
        breaks: ["<b>Approximate, and it can't tell you what it missed.</b> 95% recall is fine for recommendations, not for \"every document about this legal matter.\"", "<b>Filters.</b> \"Nearest among messages from Emily\": filter first and the structure is useless; filter after and top-k may be empty.", "<b>Updates and deletes.</b> HNSW is expensive to change; many systems rebuild periodically.", "<b>Size.</b> 4 KB per vector, so vectors get quantized, costing recall.", "<b>Garbage neighbours.</b> The index faithfully returns whatever the model put nearby."],
        line: "an approximate nearest-neighbour structure over embeddings; it trades recall for speed, fights with filters and updates, and inherits every flaw of the model that made the vectors." }
    ]},
    { id: "storage", title: "Storage under the messaging system", intro: "What's under each table. Read/write shape and dominant query · engine family · primary key order and why · secondary indexes and what each costs.", items: [
      { q: "messages",
        robby: "Talked into \"conversations per user sorted by recency as the primary key, messages as the secondary\" — a table and a secondary index that were really two tables.",
        a: "<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td>Shape</td><td>write heavy, append only; dominant read \"last 50 in X,\" almost always the newest data</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>LSM, time-window compaction</td></tr><tr><td>Key</td><td><code>conv_id</code> first (one contiguous region), <code>seq</code> descending (the newest 50 is the first 50)</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary indexes</td><td><b>none</b>. Every candidate is a separate table: inbox = participants; by-sender = a <code>(sender_id, timestamp)</code> table if a screen needs it; search = the inverted index elsewhere. Each index would be a second write on every one of 3M sends/s.</td></tr></table></div><p>Two tables, not a table and an index: a secondary index can only reorder its own table's rows, and the inbox is one row per membership with a preview on it. In Cassandra each table is its own LSM tree.</p>" },
      { q: "participants",
        robby: "\"Read-heavy, written only on membership changes\" — it's the most rewritten table in the system. \"Bitmap hash of participant IDs\" as the key — the array mistake. \"Primary key is last_read_seq\" — that's a value.",
        a: "<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td>Shape</td><td>write heavy on a hot set: every message bumps every member's <code>last_activity</code> (250 rows for a big group); <code>last_read_seq</code> on every open. Reads: \"members of X\" on send, and the inbox.</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>B-tree. In place, one version per row, the index entry moves in place when the sort key changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Key</td><td><code>(conv_id, user_id)</code>: \"who's in X\" is a prefix range; \"is Robby in X\" a point probe.</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary</td><td><code>(user_id, last_activity DESC)</code> covering <code>conv_id</code> and the preview: the inbox. Cost: one index entry deleted and reinserted per member per message. On an LSM keyed this way, the sort key changing on every bump means read-old, tombstone, append, 250× per message — the tombstone pileup that is a well-known Cassandra inbox failure.</td></tr></table></div><p>The \"removed someone, then a message went out before the table updated\" bug is a chapter 7 question (the send path reading membership in the same transaction the removal wrote), and it's a good concrete one.</p>" },
      { q: "conversations",
        robby: "Confused it with the inbox and with the message list. It's the third table.",
        a: "<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td>Shape</td><td>mixed: written once per message (the preview moves), read as point lookups (50 for an inbox render, 1 for a thread open); a hot sliver of all conversations</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>B-tree, in Postgres next to participants so a send updates both in one transaction</td></tr><tr><td>Key</td><td><code>conv_id</code> alone; opaque for groups, sorted pair for 1:1</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary</td><td>none; the only query is by ID</td></tr></table></div><p>The preview is written <b>once</b> here versus 250 times on every member's participant row. The inbox pays 50 cheap point reads to save 250 wide writes. \"Read-heavy\" alone would point at a B-tree for messages too; the shape of the writes is what decides it.</p>" },
      { q: "users and devices",
        robby: "\"Primary key user_id alone… store online/offline… B-tree because it's not in-order writes.\" The key needs the device (or devices embed); presence doesn't live here; the reason is the hot set.",
        a: "<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td>Shape</td><td>read heavy: every delivery reads all devices for each recipient; writes are logins, token refreshes, new phones</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>B-tree; small per-user set, in place, on the hot path</td></tr><tr><td>Key</td><td><code>(user_id, device_id)</code> so fan-out is one prefix scan — or <code>user_id</code> alone with devices embedded as a JSON array, probably the better call (bounded, owned, read together), with a GIN index into the JSON for the token lookup</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary</td><td><code>push_token →</code> row, for invalid-token callbacks from Apple and Google; cheap, tokens rarely change. Platform is a column, not an index.</td></tr></table></div><p><b>Presence is ephemeral state with a TTL</b>, in memory, <code>user_id → last_heartbeat</code>. The push path doesn't check it; it pushes and lets the platform hold the notification.</p>" },
      { q: "reactions (if a table)",
        a: "<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td>Shape</td><td>write heavy, appends and toggles; reads rare (tap-to-expand), since the thread reads the summary on the message row</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>LSM — or rows in the messages log with <code>kind = reaction</code>, which is what you'd do</td></tr><tr><td>Key</td><td><code>(conv_id, seq, user_id)</code>, plus <code>emoji</code> if several are allowed; prefix range = everyone who reacted; full key = the toggle</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary</td><td>none; user is in the key</td></tr></table></div><p>Keep adding columns until the key is unique for the facts the product allows. A surrogate single-column ID is possible but still needs the composite unique constraint, and it scatters a message's reactions. Key too short: Postgres errors loudly; Cassandra upserts silently and Emily's heart replaces Robby's.</p>" },
      { q: "search index",
        robby: "\"Read-heavy, rebuilt every once in a while\" — it takes ~10 writes per send, incrementally, and it's rebuilt only when the tokenizer changes.",
        a: "<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td>Shape</td><td>write heavy by volume (a posting per word); dominant read \"my messages containing X\"</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>LSM-shaped, and not by analogy: Lucene buffers postings in memory, flushes immutable <b>segments</b>, merges in the background, tombstones deletions. Elasticsearch server-side; SQLite FTS on the device if encrypted.</td></tr><tr><td>Key</td><td><code>(user_id, term) → postings of (conv_id, seq, position)</code>. User first because search is scoped to \"my messages\"; term sorted so a prefix is a slice; the posting points at the row so results render without a second index.</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary</td><td>none; it already <i>is</i> one, fed asynchronously from the event log, lagging seconds, its 10× amplification paid in a separate system</td></tr></table></div>" },
      { q: "people-you-may-know cache",
        robby: "\"Key (my user id, their user id)… LSM, append new rows and take the first 10.\" An LSM returns the newest version per key, not the last ten rows appended.",
        a: "<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td>Shape</td><td>rewritten once per user per rebuild; read once per new-chat screen; both light</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>in-memory key-value with a TTL (a B-tree table works too — whole-row rewrite is what B-trees do well)</td></tr><tr><td>Key</td><td><code>user_id</code>; the whole ranked list is the value, replaced atomically per rebuild</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary</td><td>none; it's a cache of a derived result</td></tr></table></div><p>The bitmap math (friends of friends minus already-following, weighted) is the batch job, not the cache. Dismissals and cool-downs in their own durable table, because anything that must survive a rebuild lives where the rebuild reads from.</p>" },
      { q: "One message send, from write-ahead log to final SSTable, and where the sender's own read lands",
        a: "<pre>insert 3, 7      memtable [3 7]           full → flush     L0: [3 7]\ninsert 1, 9      memtable [1 9]           full → flush     L0: [1 9] [3 7]   ← overlap, arrival order\nL0 full → merge-sort 1 3 7 9 → cut into files of 2 → L1: [1 3] [7 9]     ← disjoint, binary-searchable</pre><p>Full path: <b>log append</b> (the ack goes out here) → memtable → flush to L0 → compaction into L1. L1 isn't bigger files, it's <i>more</i> files (10× the count per level, same size); over budget, one file moves down by merging into the ~10 it overlaps. Merging is streaming (one cursor per input, a few MB of buffers), so memory is never the constraint; <b>disk</b> is, briefly, because inputs stay until the output is written.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>When the sender reads it back</th><th>Where</th><th>Cost</th></tr><tr><td>a second later</td><td>memtable</td><td>free</td></tr><tr><td>minutes later, after the flush</td><td>newest L0 file, via its Bloom filter</td><td>one read</td></tr><tr><td>hours later, after compaction</td><td>the one L1 file whose range covers <code>(conv_id, seq)</code></td><td>one read</td></tr></table></div><p>Lookup order at every level below L0: binary-search the ranges (free, in the manifest) → one candidate → its Bloom filter → read. L0 is the odd level: raw flushes, may overlap, so every file whose range covers the key gets its filter asked. That's structural, not detected. Below L0, disjointness is a guarantee compaction makes.</p>" },
      { q: "Compaction strategy for the messages table, and the property of chat that makes it easy",
        robby: "\"Defined by the primary key, so conversations stay localized.\" The key sets the order <i>inside</i> every file; compaction is which files merge, when.",
        a: "<p><b>Messages are immutable and arrive in time order.</b> Old files never need merging with new ones. Leveled would rewrite every message ~50 times through levels it will never change in; size-tiered eventually merges last year with this year for nothing. <b>Time-window compaction</b> (Cassandra's TWCS): group SSTables by the hour or day written, compact only within a window, and when the window closes the file is final. Write amplification ≈ one extra write per message, ever; expiry is deleting whole files. Cost: a conversation active for three days spans three window files, so \"last 50\" may open two or three recent, hot, cached files instead of one.</p>",
        line: "the key gives you order within a file; immutable time-ordered data gives you time-window compaction, where old files are simply never touched again." },
      { q: "\"Messages per user per day\" and \"most active conversations this month\" on a dashboard",
        robby: "\"Compute over the LSM table on a rolling window, cache counters in memory.\" Never scan the production table; and the staleness question went unanswered.",
        a: "<p><b>Source</b>: the event stream that already feeds the search index and the inbox; the columnar fact table is the rebuild source. <b>Build</b>: live counters for today (<code>(sender, day)</code>, <code>(conversation, day)</code>, incremented per event, in memory or a small B-tree table) plus a daily rollup kept 90 days (a materialized view; the month is a sum over 30 rows). <b>Top-k over a billion conversations is a real scan</b>: run once a day in the warehouse, write the top 100 to a tiny table. <b>Staleness</b>: minutes for counters, a day for the top-k, and say so up front — declaring it is what lets the feed be asynchronous.</p>" },
      { q: "The regulator's 2025 export: which index makes it a query now, and what does it cost on every send?",
        robby: "\"Add user ID and timestamp to the primary key… won't be super expensive.\" Not in the primary key (breaks the thread range), and the cost is the whole question.",
        a: "<p>A second table, <code>(sender_id, sent_at) → (conv_id, seq)</code>, written by the application on every send (not a Cassandra native secondary, which scatters). Query: prefix range on sender between the dates → pointers → a point read per pointer for bodies; a batch job for heavy senders; under encryption the second half is ciphertext and the regulator gets who and when. Cost: <b>one more write per send</b> (3M/s becomes 6M/s across two tables, to serve a query run a few times a year); ~150 TB/year × 3 for the pointers; hot partitions for broadcast senders, so bucket by <code>(sender_id, month)</code>. The strong answer asks back: is it worth doubling writes, or does an annual export run as an hour-long scan of the fact table? For an export, the project is cheaper than the index. For a \"my sent messages\" screen, the index is the only option.</p>" },
      { q: "A Bloom filter in front of the messages SSTables: what it helps, what it can't touch",
        robby: "\"Check if you have a conversation with a contact\" (a conversations-table lookup), and \"conversations with fewer than N messages\" (a filter can't count).",
        a: "<div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><td><b>Helped</b></td><td>exact-key lookups of one message — a reaction, edit, or delete finding its target; a device asking \"do I already have 1041\" during sync, which misses constantly</td></tr><tr><td><b>Not at all</b></td><td><b>the thread read</b>, \"last 50 in X\": a range on <code>seq</code>. The filter was built from full <code>(conv_id, seq)</code> keys and \"any key starting with X\" can't be hashed. Every overlapping file opens.</td></tr></table></div><p>The dominant query on the table is the one the filters can't touch. What saves it: the manifest's range check, and a <b>prefix Bloom filter</b> on <code>conv_id</code> (RocksDB's prefix extractor), so a file can say \"no messages from X in here.\"</p>" }
    ]},
    { id: "followups", title: "Interviewer follow-ups", items: [
      { q: "\"Why is Cassandra fast at writes? Don't say 'it's optimized for writes.'\"",
        robby: "\"LSM-backed, which optimizes appends but not edits or deletes.\" The label, and the misconception: edits and deletes are appends too.",
        a: "<p>A write never reads and never seeks. It appends to the commit log (one sequential write) and inserts into an in-memory sorted table; the ack goes back after the log append; no page is located or rewritten; it doesn't check whether the key exists because every write is an upsert. The memtable flushes as one sequential file; compaction merges in the background. The disk only ever sees sequential writes, and the cost of sorting and deduplicating is paid later, by compaction. An update is a new version appended; a delete is a tombstone appended. Same path, same speed; the cost is later, in stale versions and in reads finding the newest.</p>" },
      { q: "\"Your p99 read latency spikes every few minutes on the messages table. What's happening?\"",
        robby: "\"Compaction, taking CPU and disk space.\" It's disk <i>bandwidth</i>; and the diagnosis needs a confirmation and a fix.",
        a: "<p><b>Diagnosis</b>: compaction, reading several files and writing their merge, competing with foreground reads for the disk; size-tiered makes it big and periodic. Two other suspects on Cassandra: JVM garbage-collection pauses, and memtable flushes. <b>Confirm</b>: compaction throughput and pending compactions per node against p99; if pending is climbing, compaction can't keep up and every read is getting slower. <b>Fix</b>, cheapest first: rate-limit compaction; switch strategy (time-window for messages; leveled for a steady hum if writes can afford it); commit log on a separate device; hedged reads at the client so one busy node doesn't set the p99.</p>" },
      { q: "\"Why not just add an index on every column people query?\"",
        robby: "\"Then you can't do range queries.\" A B-tree index <i>is</i> a range structure.",
        a: "<p>Every index makes one query faster and every write slower. Each is a second copy written on every insert and every update to the column; ten indexes is ten extra writes per row change plus ten structures of disk and cache. Most won't be used: low-selectivity indexes get ignored by the planner because the scan is cheaper. Mutable indexed columns on an LSM force read-before-write. And more indexes means more candidate plans and more ways to choose a bad one after a statistics refresh. Indexes are for hot, selective queries; everything else is a scan, or belongs in the warehouse.</p>" },
      { q: "\"Postgres for participants. It's a B-tree. What happens on disk when I update one row?\"",
        robby: "Re-litigated array-vs-rows instead of answering the disk question.",
        a: "<ol><li><b>Write-ahead log</b> first, flushed; the commit is acknowledged here.</li><li><b>Find the page</b>: walk the primary-key B-tree, root cached, 2–3 page reads if the leaf isn't in the buffer pool (for a hot row it is).</li><li><b>Modify in memory</b> — but Postgres doesn't overwrite: it writes a <b>new row version</b> into free space on the page and marks the old one dead (multi-version concurrency).</li><li><b>Indexes, maybe</b>: no indexed column changed and it fit on the page → heap-only tuple update, no index touched; <code>last_activity</code> changed → new entry in the inbox index, old one marked dead.</li><li><b>Page to disk later</b>, whole 8 KB, by the checkpointer; first dirtying after a checkpoint also logs a full page image (torn-page protection).</li><li><b>Vacuum</b> reclaims dead versions and dead index entries — constantly, on a table taking 250 updates per group message.</li></ol><p>A split only if the page is full. And the table itself is a heap; the B-tree is the index pointing into it.</p>" },
      { q: "\"We want the analytics dashboard to be real-time. What breaks?\"",
        robby: "First: \"What do you want to measure in real time?\" and \"How do you define activity?\" — the right first moves, two clarifying questions, not three. Then: \"the LSM requires rebalancing on write\" — an LSM tree never rebalances; drop the word.",
        a: "<p>What breaks is the batch pipeline. Real-time means stream processing: a 30-day rolling window needs 30 daily buckets per conversation (30B counters, hundreds of GB, across a cluster); top-k over a billion keys continuously means a running top-100 with an approximate structure (count-min sketch + heavy hitters); in-memory counters die with the process, so checkpoint continuously; retried events double-count without exactly-once (ch. 11); and the real-time tile and the warehouse will disagree by a few messages every week. <b>Then push back</b>: \"within a second\" for a monthly top-k is a requirement nobody needs. Offer one minute — micro-batch, daily buckets in a real table, recompute top-100 every 60 s — and build the streaming version if they can name the decision that needs the second.</p>" },
      { q: "\"Explain a Bloom filter in thirty seconds and tell me its one failure mode.\"",
        robby: "A correct minute without the mechanism.",
        a: "<p>A bit array. Add a key: hash it a few ways, set those bits. Check a key: hash the same ways; any zero → definitely not in the set; all ones → probably. A few bits per key, in memory, never a false negative. <b>The one failure mode is a false positive</b>: other keys set your bits by coincidence, you're told \"maybe\" and read a file for nothing; ~10 bits/key for 1%. It can't delete, which is why you attach one to something immutable and throw it away with the file. And \"maybe\" isn't \"some time saved\": across ten files with the key in one, it's one read instead of ten; for a key in none, zero.</p>" },
      { q: "\"Would you keep the whole participants table in memory? What would you lose?\"",
        robby: "\"600 GB\" — 50 billion rows × 60 bytes is 3 TB. Check the multiplication out loud.",
        a: "<p>No. You'd lose: <b>the transaction</b> (a send updates the preview and every member's row atomically in Postgres; a separate in-memory store makes that two systems and reintroduces the outbox); <b>durability you already had</b>; <b>restart</b> (reloading 3 TB is downtime, so replicated pairs, double the RAM); and you'd gain almost nothing, because today's active rows are already in Postgres's buffer cache and the OS page cache. What does belong in memory is ephemeral state that never needed durability: presence, typing indicators, maybe the unread counter — small, hot, fine to lose, rebuilt in seconds from the stream.</p>",
        line: "durable state on disk with a cache; ephemeral state in RAM with a TTL. That split answers almost every \"should we keep it in memory\" question." }
    ]}
  ],
  takeaways: [
    "<b>An index makes one query faster and every write slower.</b> Everything else in the chapter is a special case of that sentence.",
    "<b>B-tree: one place per key, whole pages rewritten twice in random places.</b> Wins on state that churns and reads back exactly; pays on append volume, slack, and locking.",
    "<b>LSM: sequential appends now, compaction later.</b> Wins on append-heavy tables read near the write; pays on deep reads, misses, compaction stealing the disk, and hot-key churn. It never rebalances; updates and deletes are appends.",
    "<b>Compaction is the bill.</b> Size-tiered: cheap writes, fan-out reads, 2× disk. Leveled: bounded reads, 10:1 rewrites per level. Time-window: for immutable time-ordered data, old files are never touched again.",
    "<b>A Bloom filter belongs to something immutable and bounded.</b> Per file, never global; helps exact keys, hits and misses; can't do ranges, can't count, can't delete.",
    "<b>Key order decides what sits next to what.</b> Composite keys are usable from the left; the first column is locality, the second is order within it; the primary key is the uniqueness rule as columns.",
    "<b>Secondary indexes share the engine's family because they share its transaction.</b> On an LSM, a mutable indexed column forces read-before-write; on a write-heavy table, write the second table yourself and accept lag.",
    "<b>Events go to the LSM, state goes to the B-tree, analysts get a columnar copy of the events.</b> Never scan the production table for analytics.",
    "<b>Column stores compress and scan; they can't take one row at a time.</b> Keep the fact table (narrow columns, object storage), precompute cubes from it, and declare how stale each one may be.",
    "<b>In memory when the working set fits and the structures matter more than the bytes</b>, durable through a log and a replica; otherwise disk plus a cache, and the hot rows are in RAM anyway."
  ],
  applied: {
    intro: "Beelzebub's Postgres, measured against the chapter on 2026-09-18: 2.5 million recipe rows, nine indexes on them, an inverted index and a trigram index in production, a vector index deferred, and one finding fixed on the spot.",
    items: [
      { tag: "ok", q: "The recipes table is the chapter's index ladder, running",
        a: "<p><code>recipes</code>: 2,487,465 rows, <b>2,031 MB heap + 897 MB of indexes</b> (44% of the heap, nine indexes). What's on it:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Index</th><th>What it is in chapter terms</th></tr><tr><td><code>recipes_tsv_gin</code> on <code>search_tsv</code></td><td>the <b>inverted index</b>: term dictionary + postings, filled by a trigger on insert, so an Instagram-link recipe is searchable the moment it lands</td></tr><tr><td><code>recipes_title_trgm</code> (GIN, <code>gin_trgm_ops</code>)</td><td>the <b>trigram index</b>: the \"substring in the middle\" rung that duplicates one entry per character per term. Paid for here because titles are short and writes are rare — exactly the case where it's affordable</td></tr><tr><td><code>recipes_ingredients_gin</code>, <code>recipes_categories_gin</code></td><td>GIN over arrays: postings per array element, \"recipes containing chicken thigh\" without a scan</td></tr><tr><td><code>recipes_url_uniq</code> (partial), <code>recipes_book_url</code>, <code>recipes_source_id</code>, <code>recipes_status</code> (partial)</td><td>ordinary B-trees; two are partial, so rows without a URL or status cost nothing</td></tr></table></div><p>Nine indexes on one table is the \"index every column\" warning — and it's fine <i>here</i> because the table's write shape is a one-time bulk load plus a handful of inserts a week. The per-write tax is paid on ~zero writes. Same indexes on a table taking a thousand writes a second would be a problem; on this one they're the point.</p>" },
      { tag: "fix", q: "The planner thought recipes had 8 rows",
        a: "<p><code>pg_stat_user_tables</code> showed <code>n_live_tup = 8</code> for recipes and <code>2</code> for books, with <code>last_analyze</code> and <code>last_autoanalyze</code> both null: the tables had never been analyzed since the bulk load. That's the chapter's \"the planner can pick wrong\" failure with a concrete cause — no statistics, so estimates come from stale <code>pg_class</code> numbers. Ran <code>ANALYZE recipes; ANALYZE books;</code> on 2026-09-18: <code>n_live_tup</code> now 2,487,465 and 16,299. The sample plan (a <code>search_tsv @@</code> query) was already choosing the GIN Bitmap Index Scan → Bitmap Heap Scan, the chapter's index-then-heap second hop, so the risk was to <i>join</i> plans, not to search. Why autovacuum never ran it is worth a look: a COPY inside one transaction can leave the counters at zero.</p>" },
      { tag: "ok", q: "Postgres is heap plus B-trees, and the plan shows the second hop",
        a: "<pre>Limit\n  -> Bitmap Heap Scan on recipes            ← the second hop: fetch rows from the heap\n       Recheck Cond: (search_tsv @@ 'chicken & thigh')\n       -> Bitmap Index Scan on recipes_tsv_gin   ← the inverted index, 2,316 estimated matches</pre><p>The index finds the postings, builds a bitmap of heap pages, then the heap is read. No clustered index, so every index on this table pays that hop. For a 20-row limit it's a handful of page reads and doesn't matter; it's the shape the chapter describes.</p>" },
      { tag: "ok", q: "Two write shapes, one engine, correctly",
        a: "<p><code>cron_runs</code> (19,543 rows), <code>activity_log</code>, <code>listing_events</code>, <code>edge_requests</code> are append-only event tables on a B-tree — the shape the chapter says wants an LSM. At a few rows a minute the whole-page-rewrite tax is invisible, and the <code>(job, fired_at DESC)</code> index makes \"last run of job X\" a one-probe range read, which is the query the dashboard and the health email run. <code>kv</code>, <code>bills</code>, <code>places</code> are in-place state, the B-tree's home. Both shapes on one engine is right at this volume; the chapter's split only pays when the event table's writes are the bottleneck.</p>" },
      { tag: "idea", q: "The vector index row, deferred",
        a: "<p>pgvector is installed but not linked into postgresql@16 (\"Phase 3 semantic search\"). That's the chapter's last row waiting to happen: an approximate nearest-neighbour index (pgvector offers HNSW and IVF, the two named in the chapter) over recipe or place embeddings, for \"something like that Sichuan dish\" rather than a term match. Everything the row warns about applies at small scale too — recall you can't see, filters (\"nearest among owned books\") fighting the index, and garbage neighbours if the embedding model is weak. At 2.5M recipes × ~1.5 KB per 384-dim vector it's ~4 GB of vectors, so quantization would be a real decision, not a footnote.</p>" },
      { tag: "ok", q: "Durability ladder: log, snapshot, remote copy",
        a: "<p>Postgres's write-ahead log covers a crash; the weekly <code>db-backup</code> job is the snapshot (<code>pg_dump</code> gzipped), copied to Google Drive with 35-day retention, and <code>restore.py</code> is the rebuild path. That's the chapter's in-memory-durability ladder applied to a disk database: log for crashes, snapshot for disasters, a remote copy for the machine itself dying. What's missing, deliberately, is replication — there's no second machine — so recovery is measured in minutes of restore, not seconds of failover, and the trade is stated in the README.</p>" }
    ]
  }
});
